# 03. CRITICAL DOMAIN DISTINCTIONS

The following distinctions are mandatory.

## 3.1 Observation ≠ Signal

An observation is information received from a source.

A signal is an interpretation that an observation or collection of observations may warrant attention.

---

## 3.2 Signal ≠ Event

A signal indicates something potentially meaningful.

An event is a managed operational situation.

Not every signal becomes an event.

---

## 3.3 Event ≠ Threat

An event is something being investigated or managed.

A threat assessment is an interpretation of the potential significance of the event.

---

## 3.4 Threat ≠ Diagnosis

AEGIS is not inherently a clinical diagnostic system.

A threat assessment represents operational significance and uncertainty.

It must not automatically be represented as a medical diagnosis.

---

## 3.5 Evidence ≠ Assessment

Evidence supports an assessment.

Evidence itself should remain distinguishable from the conclusion derived from it.

---

## 3.6 Assessment ≠ Decision

An assessment describes the current interpretation.

A decision determines what the responsible human or organization chooses to do.

---

## 3.7 Recommendation ≠ Authorization

AEGIS may recommend an operational response.

That does not mean the response has been approved.

---

## 3.8 Authorization ≠ Execution

An approved response has not necessarily happened.

---

## 3.9 Execution ≠ Verification

An action being reported as executed does not prove that its intended outcome occurred.

---

## 3.10 Resolution ≠ Learning

Closing an event does not automatically create institutional memory.

The learning process must be explicit.

