# 29. ASSESSMENT DOMAIN

An Assessment represents the current authorized interpretation of an event.

It should answer:

> What do we currently believe this event means?

---

# 30. ASSESSMENT MODEL

```text
Assessment
├── assessment_id
├── event_id
├── assessment_type
├── classification
├── confidence
├── risk_indicator
├── rationale
├── supporting_evidence
├── contradicting_evidence
├── missing_information
├── assessor
├── status
├── created_at
├── supersedes
└── metadata
```

---

# 31. CONFIDENCE MODEL

Confidence answers:

> How strongly does the available evidence support this interpretation?

Conceptually:

```text
LOW
MODERATE
HIGH
```

The exact scale may later be formalized.

But the architecture must preserve confidence as a separate field.

---

# 32. RISK MODEL

Risk answers:

> What is the potential operational significance?

Risk must remain separate from confidence.

Therefore the domain permits:

```text
Low Confidence + High Potential Significance
```

and:

```text
High Confidence + Low Potential Significance
```

This is intentional.

---

# 33. ASSESSMENT VERSIONING

Assessments must be versioned.

Example:

```text
Assessment v1
    ↓
Assessment v2
    ↓
Assessment v3
```

The previous assessment should not be silently overwritten.

Each revision must preserve:

```text
who changed it
when
why
what evidence changed
what the new interpretation became
```

---

# 34. ASSESSMENT STATE

```text
DRAFT
↓
UNDER_REVIEW
↓
PUBLISHED
↓
SUPERSEDED
```

Possible:

```text
